chunsuzuosheng.translation:
At dusk the flowers hide by the palace wall;
Chirping, the roosting birds fly past.
Stars draw near, ten thousand doors seem to stir;
The moon leans on the ninefold sky and shines the more.
Sleepless, I listen for the golden keys;
On the wind I think of the jade bridle-bells.
Tomorrow morning there is a sealed memorial to present,
And again and again I ask how the night goes.

dengyanzhouchenglou.translation:
In the days I came to Dongjun to attend my father,
I first let my eyes roam from the South Tower.
Floating clouds join the sea to the mountains;
The flat plain runs into Qing and Xu.
On a lone peak the Qin stele still stands;
In the ruined city a hall of Lu is left.
There has always been much of the old feeling here;
Looking out, I alone linger, unable to leave.

poshansihouchanyuan.translation:
At clear dawn I enter the ancient temple;
The first sun lights the tall woods.
A winding path leads to a secluded place;
By the meditation rooms, flowers and trees grow deep.
Mountain light brings joy to the nature of the birds;
The pool's reflection empties the human heart.
All the ten thousand sounds are hushed here;
Only the note of bell and stone chime is heard.

yewang.translation:
At dusk I look out from the East Rise,
Pacing back and forth, with nowhere to lean on.
Every tree has taken on the colours of autumn;
Every hill holds only the setting light.
The herdsmen drive their calves home;
The hunters' horses come back with game.
We look at one another, and I know no one;
I sing long, and think of gathering bracken.

qiudengxuanchengxietiaobeilou.translation:
The river city is as if inside a painting;
At mountain dawn I look out at a clear sky.
Two rivers hold a bright mirror between them;
Twin bridges drop down like rainbows.
Smoke of homes makes the tangerines and pomelos look cold;
Autumn colour ages the wutong trees.
Who would think that up on the North Tower,
Facing the wind, I am longing for Master Xie?

guoxiangjisi.translation:
I did not know where Xiangji Temple was;
Some miles on, I entered peaks in the clouds.
Ancient trees, a path with no people;
Deep in the mountains, where is that bell from?
The spring sobs against the steep rocks;
The sunlight is cold on the green pines.
At dusk, by a bend of the empty pool,
I sit in meditation and subdue the poison dragon.

suyunmensige.translation:
The fragrant tower stands at the foot of Eastern Mountain;
Mist and flowers are secluded beyond the world of things.
Lamps hang over a thousand peaks at dusk;
I roll up the curtain on the Five Lakes in autumn.
On the painted wall remain the wild geese;
At the gauze window lodge the Dipper and the Ox.
I half suspect the road to heaven is near;
In dreams I wander with the white clouds.

chunrioucheng.translation:
Pale clouds, light wind, the day close to noon;
Beside the flowers, along the willows, I cross the stream ahead.
People of the day do not know the joy in my heart;
They will think I am stealing leisure to play the young man.

chunxiao.translation:
One moment of a spring night is worth a thousand in gold;
The flowers have a clear scent, the moon has shadows.
Song and pipes from the towers, thin and faint;
In the courtyard the swing hangs, the night deep and still.

chunye.translation:
The incense in the golden burner is ash, the water-clock nearly spent;
Soft breezes, gust after gust, bring chill.
The colours of spring trouble me, I cannot sleep;
The moon moves, and flower shadows climb the railing.

yuanri.translation:
Amid the sound of firecrackers the old year is cleared away;
The spring wind brings warmth into the Tusu wine.
Through a thousand gates and ten thousand doors, the sun shines bright;
Everyone puts up new peach charms in place of old.

lichunoucheng.translation:
The pitch-pipes turn, the year is late, ice and frost are fewer;
Spring comes to the human world, and plants and trees know it.
At once I feel the scene before me brimming with life;
The east wind stirs the water into uneven green.

qixi.translation:
Silver candlelight, autumn light, cold on the painted screen;
With a small fan of light silk she swats at the drifting fireflies.
The night on the palace steps is cool as water;
Lying back, she watches the Cowherd and Weaver Girl stars.

xiaochujingcisisonglinzifang.translation:
After all, this is West Lake in the sixth month;
The scenery is not like that of the other seasons.
Lotus leaves reach to the sky, endlessly green;
In the sun the lotus flowers are a different red.

xuemei.translation:
Plum and snow vie for spring and neither will give way;
The poets put down their brushes, finding it hard to judge.
The plum must yield three parts of whiteness to the snow,
But the snow loses to the plum by one measure of fragrance.
With plum and no snow, there is no spirit;
With snow and no poem, a person turns vulgar.
At dusk the poem is finished, and again the sky snows;
Together with the plum it makes a full ten parts of spring.

jiangcun.translation:
A clear river bends and flows, holding the village in its arm.
In the long summer, everything in the river village is quiet and secluded.
The swallows in the hall go and come as they please;
The gulls on the water are close and friendly.
My old wife rules paper into a chessboard;
My small son hammers a needle into a fishing hook.
Sick as I am, all I need is medicine;
What else could this humble body ask for?

xiaozhi.translation:
The seasons of heaven and the affairs of men press on one another day by day;
At the winter solstice yang is born, and spring is coming again.
Stitching the five-colour patterns, the women add a few thin threads;
In the six pitch-pipes the reed ash stirs and drifts up.
The riverbank waits for the twelfth month to let the willows unfold;
The spirit of the hills braves the cold and is about to loose the plum blossom.
Clouds and scenery are no different, but this is not my home country;
I'll have my son just drain the cup in his hand.

qiusi.translation:
Profit and desire drive men like ten thousand fire-oxen;
I drift over rivers and lakes, a single sand gull.
The long days feel like years, and I notice only when I have leisure;
Matters big as heaven are over once I am drunk.
The pounding of fulling stones fades out under the moon in the deep lane;
Paulownia leaves shake down - autumn in my old garden.
I want to stretch my old eyes but have no high place -
How could I find Yuanlong's hundred-foot tower?

dalidan.translation:
Last year I met you among the flowers, and we parted;
Today the flowers bloom again, and a year has passed.
The world is vast and hazy, hard to foresee for oneself;
In spring sorrow, dim and low, I fall asleep alone.
My body is full of illness, and I long for fields and village;
There are refugees in the district, and I am ashamed of my pay.
I hear you mean to come and ask after me -
From the west tower I have watched the moon wax full how many times?

oucheng.translation:
At leisure with nothing to do, I am unhurried in everything;
Waking from sleep, I find the east window already red with the sun.
Watching all things calmly, I see each one content in itself;
The fine moods of the four seasons are shared with all people.
The Dao runs through heaven and earth, beyond the world of form;
Thought enters the shifting shapes of wind and cloud.
Wealth and rank do not lead one astray, and poverty and lowliness bring joy -
A man who reaches this point is a hero.

chunxilvhuai.translation:
Water flows and flowers fall, both without feeling,
Seeing off the east wind as it passes the city of Chu.
In the butterfly dream, home is ten thousand li away;
On the cuckoo's branch, the moon stands at the third watch.
Letters from my old garden often go years without coming;
Spring urges on white hair, and it grows at both my temples.
I just do not go back - going back I could do at any time;
Who would contest the misty scenery of the Five Lakes?

dadingyuanzhen.translation:
I doubt the spring wind reaches the ends of the earth;
In the second month, in this mountain town, no flowers are yet to be seen.
Leftover snow weighs on the branches, yet tangerines still hang there;
Frozen thunder startles the bamboo shoots, which are about to sprout.
At night I hear the wild geese cry and homesickness rises;
Ill, I enter the new year and feel the change in the scenery.
I was once a guest among the flowers of Luoyang,
So though the wild blooms are late, I need not sigh.

qingmingriduijiu.translation:
On the hills north and south there are many graveyards;
At Qingming everyone is bustling about, sweeping graves and making offerings.
Paper ash flies up and turns into white butterflies;
Tears of blood dye the azaleas red.
At sunset foxes sleep on the grave mounds;
At night the sons and daughters return and laugh before the lamp.
In life, when there is wine one should get drunk -
Has a single drop ever reached the Nine Springs?
